WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.680
HEIDI YACOUBIAN: My
name is Heidi Yacoubian,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.340
and I am a junior art major
here at Hillsdale College.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.880
Tonight, I will be introducing
our guest speaker, Roger W.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.860
Robinson Junior.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.680
Mr. Robinson is
President and CEO

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.160
of RWR Advisory Group,
a risk management

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.620
software and open-source
intelligence firm.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.650
He is also co-founder
and chairman

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.930
of the Prague Security
Studies Institute

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.970
in the Czech Republic.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.480
Mr. Robinson earned a BA from
Duke University and an MA

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.180
from George
Washington University.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.230
He served as Senior Director of
International Economic Affairs

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.530
on Ronald Reagan's
National Security Council,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.360
where he was the
principal architect

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.690
of the secret economic and
financial strategy that

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.180
was a key component
of efforts that

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.570
led to the demise
of the Soviet Union.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.650
He later served as chairman
of the Congressional US-China

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.740
Economic and Security
Review Commission.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.570
Prior to his
government service, he

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.420
was a vice president in the
international department

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.420
of Chase Manhattan Bank
with responsibility

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.320
for loan portfolios in the
USSR, Central and Eastern

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.030
Europe, and Yugoslavia.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.520
Everyone, please join me
in welcoming Mr. Robinson

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.210
to the stage.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.050
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:29.563 --> 00:01:31.230
ROGER W. ROBINSON:
Well, I want to start

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:35.520
by thanking Larry Arnn
and all of his colleagues

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.800
at Hillsdale for this
special opportunity

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.700
to be with you tonight and
address this esteemed CCA

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.480
event.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.810
I also want to
give special thanks

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.530
to Kim Ellsworth and Matt Bell.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.630
I took the tour today, ably
executed by Nathan Lehman.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.430
It was an experience.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.650
And I just couldn't be
more delighted to be

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.810
with you tonight.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.480
And I apologize in
advance in the sense

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.850
that I'm going to give you
a fire hose of information,

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.539
much of it new and original.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:15.000
And time is a bit tight, so I'm
going to be jumping right in,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.911
if I may.

00:02:16.911 --> 00:02:20.190
And I apologize for a
little bit of a cough,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.750
but we'll try to
push through that.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.600
Before we turn to
what is clearly

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.470
China's economic and financial
warfare strategy being waged

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.190
against us, and I'll explain
the components, many of which

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:41.280
you know, some of which you
won't know until we're done,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.270
I want to get into an
earlier episode that was

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.920
alluded to in the introduction.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:52.140
Namely, the true story of
the Soviet Union's effort

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:57.270
to almost succeed in
continuing to keep itself

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:03.870
a going concern by living
off of the West, off of us,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.770
and muddling through to
this very day, all of which

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:12.210
was brought to a screeching
halt by a president

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.680
named Ronald Reagan and his
National Security Advisor

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.410
and best friend,
William P. Clark,

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.920
who I had the privilege
of serving under.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.030
And as mentioned, I had
been with Chase Bank as vice

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.660
president division
executive for the US-USSR--

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.680
sorry, the USSR-Eastern
Europe division of the bank.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.600
And in that capacity,
you can imagine

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.830
it was my job to know
where the money was.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.470
Because after all,
as a banker, you're

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.320
trying to gauge
credit-worthiness.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.340
And so what I came to
realize, something I

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.510
brought to the White
House, to the NSC,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.380
which was that these
folks had no money.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:03.450
Or what they did have
was largely from us.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.050
And let me give
you some numbers.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.270
When I went to the
National Security Council

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:15.450
in the beginning of 1982, Soviet
total hard currency income

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:19.390
was approximately
$32 billion a year,

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:23.610
which was one third of the
annual revenues of General

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.920
Motors and Exxon at the time.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.250
So you have an empire
stretching from Havana

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:35.210
to Hanoi that is the equivalent
of the revenues of one

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.450
third of one American company.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.310
And it was that
kind of circumstance

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.670
that we were looking
at, and other

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:50.910
what I would call life
support tubes that

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:56.100
were keeping them going,
notably loan arrangements.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.770
I mean, the Soviets were
spending about $16 billion more

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.930
than they were making.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:08.310
All of that $16
billion gap was being

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.790
financed by Western
governments and banks.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:17.240
We weren't funding 10%
or something like that.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:21.430
But when you think
about $16 billion,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.460
coincidentally, that
was the approximate cost

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.790
in hard currency,
remembering that the ruble is

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.730
non-convertible,
that was the cost

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.580
of the external Soviet empire.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.250
Everything outside
of the Soviet Union

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:41.510
itself was totaling
about $16 billion.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.430
So you can imagine
President Reagan,

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.890
who always believed that
the Soviets were terribly

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:54.250
vulnerable economically,
to learn that we, the West,

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:59.890
were financing 100% of
the external empire.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:03.250
It's that kind of thing,
those kind of numbers,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:05.740
that he was looking
for all this time.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.710
He knew that they had
no entrepreneurship,

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:11.500
that they had no
technological dynamism,

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.470
that they had none
of the freedoms

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.710
that you need to have a
functioning free market

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:18.500
economy.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:21.490
This was all an administered
command event, as all of you

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.540
know, fraught with rigidities.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:29.910
And so this really
came as a surprise.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.470
The Europeans, on
the other hand,

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.160
had a completely
different philosophy.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.680
They believed in
Ostpolitik, as the Germans

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.980
call it, which is that
commercial bridge-building

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.960
would lead,
inevitably, to greater

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.090
geopolitical cooperation.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.010
Gee, I wonder where
we've heard that before.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.690
You know, China, right?

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.270
We were supposed to
buy into the notion

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:55.110
that if we just gave them
WTO, and we gave them

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.560
our technology, and we gave
them our money and access

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.850
to our capital markets and
trade markets, that they, too,

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:07.860
would come around to a
political accommodation

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.260
and give their
people more freedom

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:14.070
and have a safer, more
secure world with us.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.030
Obviously didn't happen.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.360
The same was true
with Soviet detente,

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.500
the Nixon-Kissinger period.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.320
What did they do
with those credits?

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.780
What did they do
with that technology?

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.410
Of course they used
it all against us,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.730
and to make themselves stronger,
to expand their empire,

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:38.190
and to basically continue to
engage in nefarious activities.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:41.950
And so here we go again.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:49.110
So basically, where we drew
the line was on a project

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.210
called the Siberian
Gas Pipeline Project.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.610
It was a two--

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:57.690
a twin strand huge
pipeline, 3,600 miles,

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:02.363
from Siberia into the
West European gas grid.

00:08:02.363 --> 00:08:03.780
It was going to
be the centerpiece

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.080
of their future hard
currency earnings structure.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.430
For them, it was a
magnificent twofer.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:13.890
Dependency of Western
Europe on natural gas

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:19.200
would soar to over
70%, 75% minimum,

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.160
and they would nearly double
that anemic $32 billion

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.580
figure with just one deal.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.670
And this is in the midst
of their massing troops

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:34.409
on the Polish border
to invade the moment

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:37.890
that they learned that
General Jaruzelski was not

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:40.530
going to suppress Solidarity.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:45.270
He was not going to
silence John Paul II.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.260
That he was going to
declare martial law,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:53.250
or we would have yet another
Soviet-style invasion, tanks

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:54.870
and all.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.020
So it's in this window
that the largest

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.070
deal in East-West economic
history was going down.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.200
Not only was it going
down, but it was going down

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.590
on taxpayer subsidized terms.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:12.600
The Soviets were viewed as
a less developed country,

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.060
worthy of below
market interest rates.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.590
And Germany, France, others
were too happy to accommodate.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:23.220
Because for the Germans,
they had 19 million hostages

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.230
on the other side of the wall.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.780
I mean, for them, this
was a protection racket.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.420
They wanted to pay
off the Soviets

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:33.310
so that they would have
better care of their people

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:37.360
that keep the
peace at all costs.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.260
You know, throw steak
in the cage of the bear

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.630
and keep it mollified.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.730
France was no better
in this regard.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:50.680
So the president had
a decision to make.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.280
We had a monopoly on
oil and gas equipment

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.040
and technology that could
go through permafrost.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.990
We got it from the North Slope.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:01.470
Nobody else in the world
had it at the time.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:06.150
So we used that monopoly
to basically take

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:10.470
the side of our
Polish friends and try

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:14.220
to penalize the Soviets
for massing these troops

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.220
and threatening yet
another invasion.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.460
Far from doing the biggest
deal in East-West history,

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.210
the president was
dedicated to stopping it.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:26.910
And appropriately so.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:33.450
So basically, we declared oil
and gas equipment sanctions.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.940
Those contracts were
picked up by the Europeans,

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.640
who were selling
the same equipment,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.020
but under our licenses.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:44.730
Now, this is kind of a long
story, too long for tonight,

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.920
because I want to
transition over to China

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.880
as quickly as we can.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:53.340
But we need to understand that
this disconnect with Europe,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:59.520
then and now with Russia,
was a blood on the floor

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.860
estrangement at the time.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:07.080
I mean, we had to, at one
point, close the US market

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.390
entirely to those
European companies

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:13.200
that continued to ship to the
Soviet Siberian Gas Pipeline

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.220
over our objections.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.350
And we had to give
them a choice.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.020
You're going to do business with
the Soviet Union or the United

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:25.340
States, but you're not going
to do business with both.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:32.090
Well, four of the six companies
that we imposed import controls

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:38.260
against, as they're called,
went under within six months.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:43.790
And about 250,000 people went
into the street unemployed.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:46.130
Europe got the picture.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:49.790
And the epilogue of
this story, in short,

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:56.930
is that we put a cap on
Soviet gas deliveries

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.395
to Western Europe at 30%
of total supplies period.

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:06.380
We ended the subsidized credits.

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:10.760
In fact, we helped dry up
the provision of credits

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:14.030
by Western governments
and banks altogether.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.820
We went to the Saudis
secretly to have

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:22.300
them pump two million
barrels more oil a day,

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.640
and [? decontrolled ?]
prices at the wellhead

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:28.300
here, knocking oil prices
down to $10 a barrel.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.250
Knowing that for
every dollar drop

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.930
in the price of a barrel of oil,
the Soviets lost $500 million

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:38.740
to a billion per dollar.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:42.590
This is on that anemic number
that I was talking about.

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:46.930
So the strategy for the
takedown of the Soviet Union

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:49.780
was a secret one, but
nothing was more secret

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:52.600
than the economic
and financial piece.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:55.390
There were maximum 13
people in the country

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:59.440
that knew about this
strategy, because our fear was

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.290
that if the Soviet
Union understood what

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:04.600
we were about there,
and they started

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:10.410
to see their demise
loom too large,

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.060
it could be use it
or lose it time,

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.890
as we say in the nuclear trade.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.800
Back up against the
wall, the whole thing.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.100
So ironically, you'd think
that the political military

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:23.110
is always the more
secret components.

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:24.500
Wrong.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.970
It's about the money.

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:32.760
It's about economics and
finance, then and now.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.110
So with that-- oh,
and you know we're

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:37.940
looking at Nord Stream 2 today.

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:41.960
The president's calling
Germany a captive nation

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:47.790
to Russia today, when the
dependency level's about 35%.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:50.720
How would we be feeling
if that number was 80%?

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.580
Which it would have
been had there not

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:56.590
been a Reagan and a Clark.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:59.950
And to the extent that I was
able to play a role in that,

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:04.390
it's been a privilege
all my life.

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:08.920
So that's a little bit of the
epilogue that we're looking at.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:12.400
And it is relevant
to the China story,

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:18.570
because the Soviets,
if you think about it,

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.300
were doing something
rather unique.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:24.270
They were using natural
gas as a weapon.

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.750
There was no such thing
as pipeline politics

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:28.880
like there is today.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.430
They had never used
natural gas as a weapon

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.970
before to leverage
NATO and cause

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:39.230
those divisions in the alliance
that are so debilitating.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.900
We thought, and
had to speculate,

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:46.150
that if they had the
power, they would use it.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:47.290
Europe didn't think so.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:50.650
They thought that, my
gosh, if the Soviet

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:55.910
Union shows itself not to be
a reliable supplier, that's

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:56.810
crazed.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:59.550
I mean, that's just
not going to happen.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.230
Of course they were
wrong, as history

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.630
has proven, when
they could use--

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:06.630
ask Ukraine, ask
the Baltic states.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:10.380
Ask anybody who's ever crossed
the Soviets and the Russians

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:11.430
today.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:16.980
They're using natural gas
routinely as a weapon.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:23.600
So who would have thought
that, literally underground,

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:29.990
on this subterranean basis,
the tentacles of our demise

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:33.500
were being delivered?

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:41.660
Today, those tentacles are
China in our capital markets.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:45.200
Now, I'll get to that
story in a moment.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.520
Let's talk about China's
economic and financial warfare

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:52.710
against us for
just a moment here.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:55.470
You know about Belt
and Road, obviously

00:15:55.470 --> 00:16:00.870
a strategic, wildly
ambitious undertaking,

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:05.040
to wrap up huge segments of
the world on their terms.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:08.280
You know about China
2025, where they're

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.130
trying to dominate the
key technology sectors--

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.450
everything from
artificial intelligence,

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:16.630
quantum computing, you name it.

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:18.660
And they are trying
to get there first.

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:19.950
5G.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.780
You read about these things.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.450
The forced technology
transfer we know about.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:28.290
The predatory trade
practices you know about.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:33.140
The president brought these
things to the forefront

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:37.240
after countless
presidents let it slide.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.490
And he is to be given
credit for that.

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:45.190
The tariff war is a
good thing in the sense

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:49.330
that we've got to take a stand.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:52.397
But these are things you know.

00:16:52.397 --> 00:16:53.730
Here's something you don't know.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:17:03.170
China has something over 700
companies in our stock and bond

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:06.425
markets, our so-called
capital markets.

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:12.109
They've got about 86 companies
in the New York Stock Exchange,

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:17.150
about 62 in NASDAQ, and over 500
in the over-the-counter market,

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:21.560
this very murky,
poorly-regulated market.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.329
Of course they
gravitate to something

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:28.160
that's non-transparent and the
most lax regulatory regime.

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:30.300
All good police states do that.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:36.900
And among those
700 companies, we

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.770
have some of the most
egregious bad actors who've

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.470
been committing
national security

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.900
abuses against our country.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.105
The same with human
rights abuses.

00:17:48.105 --> 00:17:51.540
I mean companies
like Hikvision that

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:54.900
is responsible for
the facial recognition

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:58.350
technology and the
surveillance cameras that

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:03.510
are placed along the walls every
few meters of the concentration

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:09.660
camps holding one to two million
Uyghurs, religious minorities,

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:11.890
in Xinjiang.

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:17.160
Gee, I wonder if we had in mind
helping finance concentration

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:21.420
camps with our retirement
dollars and our investment

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.790
accounts.

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:26.420
Most of the people
in this room are.

00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:29.360
I know that that
sounds wild, but that's

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.700
an empirical fact,
in my view, if we

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:34.160
take that statement as it is.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:35.840
The majority of you.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:41.450
Unwittingly, of course, because
we have no screening mechanism

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.070
in the United States.

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:45.420
Never have for our
capital markets.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:46.670
I don't know how big they are.

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:50.360
Around $40 to $60 trillion of
funds under management or so.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.690
Look, it's not the same.

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:56.740
It's not true with trade.

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:59.140
We've got the Committee
on Foreign Investment

00:18:59.140 --> 00:19:00.730
in the United States.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:03.790
We just strengthened it further
with the so-called FIRRMA

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:05.790
legislation.

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:08.730
We're worried about
Chinese coming in there

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:12.240
and trying to take our
high technology companies,

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.100
to try to position
themselves right

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.260
next to our military bases.

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:18.850
In other words,
anything they can do.

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:21.210
We've got to be
scrutinizing this,

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:25.650
and we've belatedly woken
up and are doing so now.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.720
Belatedly.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:32.050
Well, what about the money?

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:34.360
Completely unprotected.

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:39.730
Do we really think the SEC is
screening Chinese companies

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:44.830
for a litany of their past
national security abuses?

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:47.830
If they are, they're doing a
pretty poor job, because there

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:52.120
are repeat sanctions violators.

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:56.600
There are proliferators of
weapons of mass destruction

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:59.030
and ballistic missiles.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:04.730
There are advanced weapons
manufacturers for the People's

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:06.820
Liberation Army.

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:11.590
There are South China Sea island
builders and militarizers.

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:17.480
There are companies underwriting
the North Korean threat.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:20.060
There are known hackers.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.220
We know who they are.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:24.435
They're here in our
capital markets.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:29.130
There are companies
accused of espionage,

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:32.610
that have been indicted or
had their employees indicted.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:35.250
Do you find any of
this in the risk

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:37.800
section of the prospectuses?

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:40.500
Are any of you
hearing about this

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:44.250
from your financial planners
and your fund managers?

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:48.630
I mean, have you ever seen
one risk communicated to you

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:51.060
of who these folks
really are, and who

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:54.270
their network of
subsidiaries are?

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:55.320
No.

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:56.160
Not a word.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:58.590
Not one of you, ever.

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:00.630
Have you ever heard
this topic discussed

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:05.370
before at a cocktail party,
much less on television,

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:10.230
a think tank session, a session
like the one we're having now?

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:11.130
Guess what.

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:14.010
There's not been one
interagency meeting

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:15.550
in the executive branch.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:18.180
There's not been one
hearing in the Congress.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:22.790
Ever.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:25.930
Now, what does that tell you?

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:29.170
What it should tell
you is that China

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:33.550
wants us to concentrate on
this shiny object over here

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.280
called trade.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:38.160
They can do tariffs
all day long.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:40.140
I mean, they're
not-- it's hurting

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:43.950
them, which is a good
thing, but not that badly.

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:47.500
In other words,
I'm talking tonight

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:51.720
about what they never
want you to see.

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:55.090
What they desperately
need is the dollars.

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:57.090
I mean, they have practically
a trillion dollars

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.100
of exposure in our markets.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:02.400
We don't even know how
much, because they've

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:06.060
got dollar-denominated
bonds, our currency,

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:09.640
being issued in Hong Kong,
Frankfurt, Singapore.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.820
Well, guess where
they're ending up?

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:17.070
In your bond portfolios, because
our investment banks buy them

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:20.610
from overseas to get
around the loopholes

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.430
of regulatory
regimes, and in they

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:25.630
come in the secondary market.

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:28.200
So we don't even know
what our exposure to China

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:31.440
is, but I can tell you this.

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:37.620
In the next 36 months, it'll
be about $2 to $3 trillion

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:40.680
more than it is today.

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:42.980
I mean, they are
moving as fast as they

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:46.980
can into your portfolios.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.820
I don't know how many
people in America

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:51.380
are in the capital markets.

00:22:51.380 --> 00:22:52.910
Let me give you a rough guess.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:57.210
150 million, 180 million of us.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:59.960
You know, older folks, babies.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:01.790
The Gerber program.

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:05.130
I mean, this is all
stocks and bonds, right?

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:10.420
So what if we wake
up one morning

00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:17.290
and we find out 14%, 17%, 22%
of our retirement accounts

00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:20.980
are in Chinese securities?

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:23.790
Now, that may seem
far-fetched to you.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.630
I suggest to you that's
what's going to happen.

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:28.690
And that's soon.

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:30.070
Now when that happens--

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.210
if it does--

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:36.400
I pray it doesn't, but these are
trends that are pretty clear.

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:37.930
I mean, Goldman
Sachs and others are

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:42.010
the ones that are predicting
this, at least on the numbers,

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:43.520
you know, on how
much is coming in.

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:50.230
Well then the Chinese
have figured out

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:55.520
that all of these scores
of millions of Americans

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:58.790
have a vested financial
interest in opposing

00:23:58.790 --> 00:24:00.950
any future sanctions
against them,

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:04.070
in opposing any penalties
whatsoever against them,

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:07.265
irrespective of the
severity of the offenses.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:14.190
That's called checkmate,
ladies and gentlemen.

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:18.290
That's called an
exponential increase

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:21.150
in the scale of the
so-called China lobby.

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:24.750
We think that the China
lobby's very formidable today.

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:27.960
Lord knows it is.

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:33.510
That's kiddie cars next
to where we're headed.

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:35.910
And we're-- it's
not talked about.

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:38.130
We're not seeing
it any more than we

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:49.240
saw the subterranean natural gas
strangling our European allies,

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:52.820
and undermining the
structure of NATO.

00:24:52.820 --> 00:24:55.390
So here we go again.

00:24:55.390 --> 00:25:01.030
Another unseen villain in
the economic and financial

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:03.910
portfolio.

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:13.840
I mean, let me give you a live,
happening right now, event.

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:18.850
Well, first I
should say that it's

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:23.470
even the case that China's
sovereign bonds are here.

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:26.440
That Russia has
sovereign bonds here.

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:27.940
When I say sovereign
bonds, I mean

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:33.270
these are bonds initiated
by the government.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:35.730
Remember our Liberty
bonds during World War II?

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.490
I mean, we used to
finance our war effort

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.050
with bonds which the
government, of course,

00:25:41.050 --> 00:25:43.660
can use at their
sole discretion.

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:48.250
Well now, our
countrymen are buying

00:25:48.250 --> 00:25:54.860
Chinese and Russian bonds
to destroy our liberties.

00:25:54.860 --> 00:26:00.680
I mean, do you know that
CalPERS owns $460 million

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:05.050
in Russian sovereign
bonds by itself?

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:10.610
Just do the math as to what
the other 50 states have.

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:14.180
I mean, when we talk
about the 50 states,

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:17.300
we've looked at probably
20 of those states.

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:21.110
They're littered with Chinese
bad actors of the type

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:22.090
that I've described.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:27.650
When you look at university
endowments and college

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:32.630
endowments, same.

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:37.050
I mean, University of
Michigan, for example,

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:45.840
has 44% of its $12.2 billion
in assets in private equity

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.350
and venture capital.

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:51.050
That's a new, big
shift in that direction

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:54.690
from University of Michigan.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:58.350
Of the venture capital
portion, one third are Chinese.

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:04.530
Now, I chose a local example.

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:06.530
And it's not like I'm
trying to excoriate

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:08.480
University of Michigan.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:09.590
I'm not.

00:27:09.590 --> 00:27:11.600
This could be any of us.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:13.590
That's the problem.

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:18.770
I mean, I'm not here to point
fingers at them, because again,

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:20.210
where's the disclosure?

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:24.860
But also, I have to say
they're not blame-free.

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:27.690
Where's their diligence?

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:30.600
I mean, they have all kinds
of rules, and standards,

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:35.100
and governance that on paper
say that they're not going to be

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:37.740
financing concentration camps.

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:40.650
And they're not going to
be funding and underwriting

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:43.320
the People's Liberation
Army and folks

00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:48.420
like that, or folks engaged
in espionage and US sanctions

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:49.200
violations.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:51.730
I mean, really.

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:54.850
But it's happening.

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:57.000
So we have a problem
in the 50 states.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:58.740
Let me tell you.

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:02.005
State legislatures need
to take this up ASAP.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.810
So that's where we sit.

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:12.010
Now, take your live example.

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:13.900
Now, this is private
capital for the most

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:16.700
part we're talking about.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:20.260
Let's talk about
our taxpayer money.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:23.830
For the longest time, the
federal Thrift Savings

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:29.810
Plan in this country, which is
the federal retirement system

00:28:29.810 --> 00:28:31.970
for all federal employees.

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:37.940
All members of the military,
the cabinet, the Congress,

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:43.670
the Joint Chiefs, our combatant
commanders, our fighting men

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:48.200
and women, are all relying
on the Thrift Savings Plan.

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:52.590
$578 billion-- the biggest in
the country that I know of.

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:55.030
CalPERS is $370.

00:28:55.030 --> 00:28:59.490
That's the biggest pension
fund, but this is bigger.

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:06.060
5.7 million people, not
surprising, are covered.

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:08.890
So I was checking on them.

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:11.920
Well, for the longest time,
they were doing the right thing.

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:16.420
They would-- they have an
international portfolio.

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:17.380
It's $50 billion.

00:29:19.950 --> 00:29:23.400
For the longest time,
they were using an index.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:25.950
The Morgan Stanley
Capital investment

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:30.120
has a whole range of
indexes, but this one

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:35.940
was developed countries, largely
industrialized democracies.

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:40.380
Well, OK, international
exposure, diversification,

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:43.190
all that's not a bad thing.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:45.670
But guess what?

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:49.450
In November or
December of 2017, they

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:52.120
had the bright idea
of making a change.

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:55.960
They wanted to capture those
yields of the emerging market,

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:59.320
so they hired a consulting
firm, an investment consultant

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:01.630
from Wall Street,
and said we want

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:03.850
to expand the
universe of companies

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:05.910
we can own internationally.

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:07.270
Well, they said fine.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:09.710
Have we got a deal for you.

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:14.335
The Morgan Stanley, the MSCI,
All Country World Index.

00:30:18.250 --> 00:30:21.650
Well, that includes China.

00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:26.280
And I know for a fact
that it includes many

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:28.800
of these egregious bad actors.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.750
I could name them.

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:35.230
I mean, we're talking
about companies like AVIC.

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:36.220
That's PLA.

00:30:36.220 --> 00:30:38.290
They make fighter aircraft.

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:42.530
I mean, we're talking about
some of these indexes own China

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:43.480
shipbuilding.

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:47.540
They make ballistic
missile submarines

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:50.390
targeting our cities.

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.040
And the list goes on.

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:54.890
Not to mention, again,
the facial recognition

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:56.420
and surveillance state.

00:30:56.420 --> 00:31:02.760
This digital, totalitarian,
police state innovation,

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.900
in their minds.

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:08.110
Well, I don't think we have
a mind financing that kind

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:11.010
of innovation for China, right?

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:13.320
Not with our money.

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:16.950
So this is going
to be implemented.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:18.960
And by the way,
that's going to be

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:20.680
all of the international
portfolios.

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:22.530
It's not like you're
going to have a choice.

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.810
If you have any
international exposure,

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:29.010
you're in the MSCI All
Country World Index.

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:35.630
And you own China automatically,
and the bad actors there too.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:40.390
This goes into effect in 2020.

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:41.390
Right around the corner.

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:45.690
The decision's already made,
yet it's not been implemented.

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:49.190
We've got to reverse
that decision.

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:52.550
Either that, or Nancy
Pelosi, who cares deeply

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:55.340
about the Tibetans,
with her money,

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:57.410
is going to be
financing, or is going

00:31:57.410 --> 00:32:02.090
to own a company, that is
doing the facial recognition

00:32:02.090 --> 00:32:07.100
technology to be able to pick up
the Tibetans at train stations

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:09.210
and incarcerate them.

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:11.610
Again, the same
with the Uyghurs.

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:14.460
Any champion of
human rights is going

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:18.850
to be violating
their sacred values.

00:32:18.850 --> 00:32:22.600
The Joint Chiefs of Staff,
our combatant commanders,

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:24.880
our Secretary of
Defense, they're

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:27.430
going to be underwriting
the militarization

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:30.370
of the illegal islands
in the South China Sea.

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:34.810
Not conceptually, with
their retirement dollars.

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:37.210
The Secretary of State
of the United States,

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:39.950
if he has
international exposure,

00:32:39.950 --> 00:32:43.900
he is going to be financing
multiple US sanctions

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:45.460
violators.

00:32:45.460 --> 00:32:49.490
Gee, that'll go down
well politically.

00:32:49.490 --> 00:32:54.940
So if we can't stop this,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:32:54.940 --> 00:32:57.190
we're in a very, very bad way.

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:00.460
You know, we can lose
this thing to China.

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:01.750
I mean, they have no cushion.

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:06.160
They're a lot more frail
and weak than we think.

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:10.450
But again, like the Soviets,
if we keep these life support

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:14.950
tubes pumping, we're going
to finance our way out

00:33:14.950 --> 00:33:19.480
of our freedoms, out of our way
of life, and out of everything

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:22.210
we hold dear.

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:24.010
It's the money.

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:26.470
It was the money on the Soviets.

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:30.450
It's the money on China.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:34.660
That's what they don't
want you to know.

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:36.505
If you go and ask
your fund manager

00:33:36.505 --> 00:33:40.690
or your financial planner,
gee, do I own China?

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:43.450
I mean, do I own
Chinese companies?

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:46.480
They're going to look at you
quizzically and probably say,

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:49.210
you know, I don't really know.

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:52.540
And if you ask, well, what
kind of Chinese companies?

00:33:52.540 --> 00:33:55.870
I mean, have you guys looked
at the parent companies

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:57.010
and their subsidiaries?

00:33:57.010 --> 00:34:00.400
And do they have a track record
of national security and human

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:02.730
rights abuses?

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:07.590
You're going to have a
blank stare coming back.

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:11.500
No way you're going
to get that answer.

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:13.360
What does that tell you?

00:34:13.360 --> 00:34:16.880
I'm telling you, they're not
going to answer the question.

00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:19.600
So you tell me what that
says about the state

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:23.335
of play in your own lives
and the life of our nation.

00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:33.679
So our detractors, of arguments
like mine, are saying, well,

00:34:33.679 --> 00:34:36.940
this guy Robinson
and people like him,

00:34:36.940 --> 00:34:41.170
I mean, they're going
to be-- their views

00:34:41.170 --> 00:34:45.260
are detrimental to the free
flow of global capital.

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:48.120
They're trying to contract
our investable universe.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:50.449
They're trying to
narrow what we can own

00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:55.199
and what we can buy as we
try to get a better yield.

00:34:55.199 --> 00:34:58.535
They're going to say we're
politicizing the markets.

00:34:58.535 --> 00:34:59.660
What's it going to be next?

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:01.940
Tobacco, guns.

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:04.730
You know, they're
comparing national security

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:10.360
and human rights to socially
responsible investing.

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:11.540
These aren't preferences.

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:14.680
These are material risks.

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:16.810
They are undisclosed.

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:18.640
That's legally actionable.

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:22.210
It's called material
risk omission.

00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:24.040
You're not allowed to do that.

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:27.445
That's what class action
lawsuits are made of.

00:35:27.445 --> 00:35:29.320
And that's, by the way,
one of the directions

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:31.030
we're going to go.

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:33.680
Now, I could give you,
maybe in the Qs and A,

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:38.740
some ideas about what we can
do, but let me give you--

00:35:38.740 --> 00:35:40.870
I've given you a
lot of bad news.

00:35:40.870 --> 00:35:42.820
Let me give you some good news.

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:43.690
[? Kind ?] of.

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:44.560
And it is good news.

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:52.520
We dominate the economic
and financial domain

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:55.780
on the planet Earth.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:36:00.070
Our capital markets
are roughly the size

00:36:00.070 --> 00:36:01.570
of the rest of the
world's combined.

00:36:04.460 --> 00:36:07.007
One could say if we're making
it difficult for China,

00:36:07.007 --> 00:36:08.840
they're just going to
go to another exchange

00:36:08.840 --> 00:36:10.820
somewhere else.

00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:13.850
And we're just going to be the
ones left out of those fees.

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:17.420
Of course, this is what
Wall Street's thinking.

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:19.370
There's nowhere else to go.

00:36:19.370 --> 00:36:24.090
Nobody has the depth and
volume of our markets.

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:28.640
China's needs for dollars
are so voracious that they

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:30.980
would use up the
volume in a Frankfurt

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:37.190
or a London months, maybe.

00:36:37.190 --> 00:36:39.680
There's only us.

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:42.760
You know, we had that oil and
gas equipment and technology

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:46.390
monopoly during the Soviet
days because of North Slope.

00:36:46.390 --> 00:36:49.270
Guess what our near
monopoly is today?

00:36:49.270 --> 00:36:52.770
We have all the money.

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:55.020
They're playing in our sandbox.

00:36:55.020 --> 00:36:58.690
We're not playing in theirs.

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:01.980
These guys are waging
economic and financial warfare

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:03.300
against us.

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:06.870
We're not only not on
the field, but we're not

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:09.120
even at the stadium.

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:12.240
Now, President Trump is
trying to put us there

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:13.860
on the field on trade.

00:37:17.991 --> 00:37:20.840
But China is OK with that.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:22.820
You can throw them
into that briar-patch.

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:28.130
You don't see-- you won't
see any kind of gamesmanship

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:30.060
on the money.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:33.490
That's what they
don't want us to know.

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:40.060
So it is within our power
to take action here.

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:48.220
And all I can say is, and I want
to leave time for questions,

00:37:48.220 --> 00:37:54.650
we just have to remember, both
as taxpayers and individuals,

00:37:54.650 --> 00:38:01.270
this is our money, and
we don't have to play.

00:38:01.270 --> 00:38:04.700
I mean, Article 7 of the
National Intelligence

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:09.920
Law of China allows every single
entity, commercial entity,

00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:14.510
every enterprise, to
be weaponized instantly

00:38:14.510 --> 00:38:18.440
if Beijing orders it so-- to
commit espionage, technology

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:20.090
theft, anything they need.

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:22.040
That's a matter of record.

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:24.030
That's public.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:24.900
OK?

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:29.690
So for those that don't
want to try to differentiate

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:34.490
good Chinese from bad Chinese,
meaning seemingly commercial

00:38:34.490 --> 00:38:40.250
benign companies
from bad actors who

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:48.100
have a track record of
doing egregious harm,

00:38:48.100 --> 00:38:51.560
one solution is
to say no, China.

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:57.530
Well, that's a fairly
straightforward message

00:38:57.530 --> 00:39:00.960
that a fund manager or a
financial planner can--

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:03.760
that could penetrate.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:06.790
Anyway, we're
playing the game here

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:09.070
and trying to do this
in a constructive way.

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:12.438
If you look at
market principles,

00:39:12.438 --> 00:39:14.480
and when you're talking
to the Wall Street types,

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:17.210
and when you're talking to
$40 to $60 trillion of funds

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:21.800
under management, if we go
with outrage in our hearts

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:25.820
about these, you know,
how could this be?

00:39:25.820 --> 00:39:27.020
And how could you do this?

00:39:27.020 --> 00:39:28.580
It's unpatriotic.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:30.110
It's not the right thing.

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:33.230
You're undermining our country,
our values, our moral compass.

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:35.480
Do you know what you're going
to get from Wall Street?

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:40.190
A big yawn.

00:39:40.190 --> 00:39:45.260
But if we go to them and say,
where's the risk management?

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:47.630
Where's the disclosure?

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:49.340
Where's the transparency?

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:51.110
Where's the good governance?

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:54.110
Where's the preservation
of share value,

00:39:54.110 --> 00:39:55.820
and corporate
reputation, and brand?

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:00.710
Well, that's a lot
more uncomfortable,

00:40:00.710 --> 00:40:07.300
because that's their lingo,
and they can't ignore that.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:09.450
I mean, the SEC
is again supposed

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:13.140
to demand the disclosure
of material risk.

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:15.990
They don't consider
national security abuses

00:40:15.990 --> 00:40:18.210
and human rights
abuses of the scale

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:20.940
I'm talking about
as material risks.

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:24.870
And yet there is example after
example of Chinese companies

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:28.830
whose stock tanked as soon as
this press became available.

00:40:28.830 --> 00:40:32.970
For example, the
surveillance cameras

00:40:32.970 --> 00:40:35.520
on the concentration camps.

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:37.140
Their stock fell.

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:41.310
I mean, that's what a
material risk is all about.

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:45.020
So it's not as though I'm
asking for the world here.

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:48.810
We can ask for enhanced
disclosure, for goodness sake.

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:53.270
I mean, how minimist and how
benign does that sound to you?

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:57.940
It almost makes me seem
like I'm not a serious guy.

00:40:57.940 --> 00:41:01.070
Except there is
one small kicker.

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:04.340
The Chinese aren't
disclosing anything.

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:07.470
They're not going to
acknowledge these risks.

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:09.390
They're not going to
acknowledge disputes.

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:14.920
They're not going to acknowledge
a history of wrongdoing.

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:18.340
Because if they do, literally or
figuratively, they're taken out

00:41:18.340 --> 00:41:21.520
and shot back in Beijing.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:23.410
Again, figuratively, perhaps.

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:24.550
Lose your job.

00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:27.520
I don't know what it
may be, but you're not

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:31.830
going to see folks volunteering
to make such disclosures.

00:41:31.830 --> 00:41:33.840
And I've got an example
or two for people

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:37.390
that want to hear about
what disclosure can do.

00:41:37.390 --> 00:41:40.730
So we have the tools.

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:46.910
And we've got the enforcement
of market principles.

00:41:46.910 --> 00:41:50.210
We don't have to say, hey,
we want all Chinese excised

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:50.960
from our markets.

00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:52.400
We want sanctions.

00:41:52.400 --> 00:41:57.480
We want market closure,
or select market closure.

00:41:57.480 --> 00:41:59.710
We don't have to do that.

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:01.430
Of course that's
when they argue.

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:04.490
Oh my gosh, the
sky's going to fall.

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:06.100
No, no, no.

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:08.860
Let's just go for
the enforcement

00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:14.650
of market principles and
then see what they say.

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:16.390
Because when they
say they're not

00:42:16.390 --> 00:42:19.990
going to engage in enhanced
disclosure, that tells you all

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:21.430
you need to know.

00:42:21.430 --> 00:42:23.380
What are they hiding?

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.620
It's that old refrain.

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:29.030
So I want to leave
time for questions.

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:37.170
But again, this is our
money, and these trillions

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:47.560
are not going to be funneled
for the demise of our country,

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:53.190
the gutting of our values,
the destruction of all

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:56.470
we hold dear.

00:42:56.470 --> 00:43:01.660
Not on my watch, and
not on your watch.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:02.160
Thank you.

00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:06.516
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:14.140 --> 00:43:14.800
Please.

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:16.270
SPEAKER: Thank
you, Mr. Robinson.

00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:18.200
We now have time
for a few questions.

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.082
Please make your way
to the microphone

00:43:20.082 --> 00:43:21.040
if you have a question.

00:43:24.620 --> 00:43:26.110
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:30.222
Morgan Stanley Capital Index.

00:43:30.222 --> 00:43:32.680
ROGER W. ROBINSON: Investment,
I think it is, but yes, it's

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:34.240
the Morgan Stanley Index.

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:35.590
AUDIENCE: That's right.

00:43:35.590 --> 00:43:38.704
Morgan Stanley we know.

00:43:38.704 --> 00:43:42.910
Capital Group owns
American funds.

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:44.218
Be careful.

00:43:44.218 --> 00:43:45.260
ROGER W. ROBINSON: Right.

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:51.540
I'm not sure I quite
deciphered that, but--

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:56.292
AUDIENCE: Capital Group
owns American funds.

00:43:56.292 --> 00:43:58.160
ROGER W. ROBINSON: Oh.

00:43:58.160 --> 00:43:59.000
OK.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:01.700
Well, it might be a
nice touch if they

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:07.850
engage in some diligence and
protect the American investor

00:44:07.850 --> 00:44:11.630
from material risks to share
value and corporate reputation.

00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:14.120
That's my answer.

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:17.360
SPEAKER: We have a question
to the speaker's right.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:19.850
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:44:19.850 --> 00:44:24.320
It's the first actionable thing
I've heard at this conference,

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:25.920
and I'd like to do something.

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:30.020
So do you have a roadmap of
what you just commented on

00:44:30.020 --> 00:44:34.970
that I can follow with my
investment councilor to get--

00:44:34.970 --> 00:44:38.360
change, at least in
my portfolio, relative

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:41.890
what you're talking about?

00:44:41.890 --> 00:44:44.580
ROGER W. ROBINSON:
We are working on it.

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:48.220
This has been such an intense
three years of research.

00:44:48.220 --> 00:44:51.430
And if you can believe it, this
little Prague Security Studies

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:54.220
Institute of mine,
and, to be frank,

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:56.905
our private sector
company as well,

00:44:56.905 --> 00:45:00.220
we're the only think tank in
the world that's pursuing--

00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:01.450
that I know of--

00:45:01.450 --> 00:45:04.740
that's pursuing this issue.

00:45:04.740 --> 00:45:05.980
I mean, think about that.

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:09.840
So you can imagine that
our bandwidth is just

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:11.310
stretched to the max.

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:17.040
So we want to be able to
service just this kind of need.

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:22.040
We are developing a list
on the private sector

00:45:22.040 --> 00:45:25.520
side of Chinese bad actors.

00:45:25.520 --> 00:45:30.070
And we're way down the road
in successfully doing that.

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:35.450
A track record, a risk
profile, for all of these guys,

00:45:35.450 --> 00:45:37.580
and what they and
their subsidiaries

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:40.730
have done in these
two material risk

00:45:40.730 --> 00:45:44.010
categories of national
security and human rights.

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:48.890
So that's going to be something
that would be available.

00:45:48.890 --> 00:45:56.180
And we're looking into, again,
the 50 states, the university

00:45:56.180 --> 00:45:57.140
endowments.

00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:01.280
Right now, we're researching
the official US sanctions list

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:04.400
to find out how many sanctioned
companies are in our markets.

00:46:04.400 --> 00:46:07.640
We're looking at the entity
list of the Commerce Department.

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:08.910
Same purpose.

00:46:08.910 --> 00:46:13.010
In other words, how
widespread is this problem?

00:46:13.010 --> 00:46:15.200
It's a little bit
like trying to detect

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:20.960
what level of metastasization
do we have going on here,

00:46:20.960 --> 00:46:22.700
if you want to use
the cancer analogy.

00:46:22.700 --> 00:46:28.020
So the answer is we're
working hard now,

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:33.080
and we can be in touch to
be sure, as to helping you

00:46:33.080 --> 00:46:37.580
be better informed about the
questions, specific questions

00:46:37.580 --> 00:46:40.210
that you want to be asking.

00:46:40.210 --> 00:46:43.370
And also to search
your own portfolio

00:46:43.370 --> 00:46:48.150
to find out where are going
to be the problem areas?

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:50.570
I mean, it's not just MSCI.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:54.500
The FTSE Russell
Index, in June, they

00:46:54.500 --> 00:46:58.550
bought 1,097 Chinese
companies right out

00:46:58.550 --> 00:47:02.240
of the mainland Xinjiang
and Shanghai exchanges

00:47:02.240 --> 00:47:07.640
and added them to their
Emerging Market Index.

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:09.140
How many of those
are bad actors?

00:47:09.140 --> 00:47:10.370
We know several.

00:47:10.370 --> 00:47:13.470
We've already done
the research on that.

00:47:13.470 --> 00:47:16.740
Bloomberg Barclays
Aggregate Global Index,

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:18.390
I think it's called.

00:47:18.390 --> 00:47:21.540
$54 trillion of funds
under management.

00:47:21.540 --> 00:47:26.640
They're buying Chinese sovereign
bonds, and Chinese bank

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:30.060
and corporate bonds, and
adding those to its index.

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:32.830
As I say S&amp;P is following suit.

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:35.370
This is the new big thing.

00:47:35.370 --> 00:47:40.260
So the index providers
are the inscrutable,

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:47.150
but most problematic, because
when they buy, it's viral.

00:47:47.150 --> 00:47:50.080
I mean, they're infecting
every healthy cell

00:47:50.080 --> 00:47:54.920
of the American body
politic overnight.

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:58.990
So when folks are buying Chinese
companies left and right,

00:47:58.990 --> 00:48:08.770
in over 1,000 in a
shot, you are picking up

00:48:08.770 --> 00:48:12.000
all kinds of egregious players.

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:16.020
So we'll work with you best
we can, and anybody else

00:48:16.020 --> 00:48:21.420
here, in trying to give you
the tools that you need.

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:23.250
And we're trying to
develop those tools

00:48:23.250 --> 00:48:24.178
as quickly as we can.

00:48:24.178 --> 00:48:26.220
I mean, we've been
concentrating on trying to get

00:48:26.220 --> 00:48:28.390
the scope of the problem.

00:48:28.390 --> 00:48:31.770
But now, it's time to worry
about the solution set, so

00:48:31.770 --> 00:48:32.685
thank you.

00:48:32.685 --> 00:48:38.130
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
available and how can I get it?

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:39.850
ROGER W. ROBINSON: OK.

00:48:39.850 --> 00:48:42.100
Well, there's two ways.

00:48:42.100 --> 00:48:43.000
You can contact--

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:45.170
I mean, this isn't to
be an advertisement,

00:48:45.170 --> 00:48:52.360
but rwradvisory.com
is the corporate side,

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:56.310
pssi.cz is the think tank side.

00:48:56.310 --> 00:48:58.570
We're trying to
do some research,

00:48:58.570 --> 00:49:01.840
and we're trying to do the
activist piece on the think

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:02.920
tank side.

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:08.080
So I would say that we're good
to go within the next 60 days.

00:49:10.730 --> 00:49:15.720
And the folks here at Hillsdale
can give you our contact info.

00:49:15.720 --> 00:49:21.100
And we will answer these
questions directly,

00:49:21.100 --> 00:49:25.030
even though I seem to
be dodging them now.

00:49:25.030 --> 00:49:25.780
I mean, you know.

00:49:25.780 --> 00:49:30.250
We're not-- we're trying to do
this on our own, pretty much

00:49:30.250 --> 00:49:31.780
our own dime.

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:36.550
So it's been a tough round,
but we're getting there.

00:49:39.120 --> 00:49:41.070
And I hope-- oh.

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:42.660
I mean, we're not
keen on being alone.

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:48.860
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for coming here, sir.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:52.700
I appreciate that you're calling
out the human rights violations

00:49:52.700 --> 00:49:54.680
that China is doing,
especially to the Uyghurs

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:56.210
and the Tibetans.

00:49:56.210 --> 00:49:59.930
And I was curious if what
you're talking about,

00:49:59.930 --> 00:50:02.600
of how to deal with that and
to stop their human rights

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:07.220
violations, if you would say
you're advocating for something

00:50:07.220 --> 00:50:10.340
similar to the Boycott,
Divest, and Sanctions

00:50:10.340 --> 00:50:13.760
movement to free
Palestinians but for China?

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:16.490
Something like-- is that
what you're talking about?

00:50:16.490 --> 00:50:17.960
Economic sanctions like that?

00:50:17.960 --> 00:50:21.600
ROGER W. ROBINSON:
No, I wouldn't say so.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:25.610
I mean, we're not
calling for a boycott.

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:28.880
We're actually not even
calling, yet, for divestment.

00:50:28.880 --> 00:50:33.530
I mean, clearly there are
companies in these portfolios

00:50:33.530 --> 00:50:34.700
that shouldn't be there.

00:50:34.700 --> 00:50:38.000
And I can't believe that
the Indexes are hanging

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:39.410
in there the way they are.

00:50:39.410 --> 00:50:42.350
It's been a callous
performance, to say the least,

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:43.250
on their part.

00:50:43.250 --> 00:50:46.010
I mean, they should be dumping
some of these companies,

00:50:46.010 --> 00:50:49.160
because they've gotten a
tremendous amount of press.

00:50:49.160 --> 00:50:51.770
And Hikvision,
their parent company

00:50:51.770 --> 00:50:54.920
is on the entity list for
national security abuses.

00:50:54.920 --> 00:50:57.320
They've been banned from
federal procurement.

00:50:57.320 --> 00:51:00.320
They themselves are headed
for the entity list.

00:51:00.320 --> 00:51:02.060
Did MSCI drop them?

00:51:02.060 --> 00:51:02.840
Divest them?

00:51:02.840 --> 00:51:04.340
No.

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:09.350
So we're leaving it to folks
to pressure, including states,

00:51:09.350 --> 00:51:12.320
to pressure the index
funds, but we're not

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:15.860
doing an instant replay
of the Israeli thing.

00:51:15.860 --> 00:51:21.170
And we certainly see no
remote comparison there.

00:51:21.170 --> 00:51:25.340
We're trying to do this, as I
say, through market principles

00:51:25.340 --> 00:51:28.220
so we can be as little--

00:51:28.220 --> 00:51:32.630
to have as little disruption
to the competitiveness

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:37.600
of our exchanges and the
free flow of capital.

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:40.420
And we're not trying to
gratuitously politicize

00:51:40.420 --> 00:51:41.910
our markets.

00:51:41.910 --> 00:51:44.020
I mean, this is a
risk consideration.

00:51:44.020 --> 00:51:46.803
And China and Russia, gee,
where did we find those?

00:51:46.803 --> 00:51:48.220
I'll tell you where
we found them.

00:51:48.220 --> 00:51:50.860
The national security
strategy of the United States.

00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:53.440
The national defense strategy
of the United States.

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:56.530
What two countries have
been identified explicitly

00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:59.370
by the Trump administration
as the primary adversaries

00:51:59.370 --> 00:52:00.640
of the United States?

00:52:00.640 --> 00:52:02.080
Gee, I wonder what they are.

00:52:02.080 --> 00:52:04.790
China and Russia.

00:52:04.790 --> 00:52:08.680
China-- Russia has 25
companies in our markets.

00:52:08.680 --> 00:52:13.150
Seven of the 25 are
under US sanctions.

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:16.820
You can't do business with
them with a 100-foot pole.

00:52:16.820 --> 00:52:18.730
But you can invest in them.

00:52:18.730 --> 00:52:22.390
You can fund them with impunity.

00:52:22.390 --> 00:52:25.160
How does that work,
ladies and gentlemen?

00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:25.660
You know?

00:52:25.660 --> 00:52:28.670
Where's the consistency there?

00:52:28.670 --> 00:52:30.830
This is the disconnect
I'm talking about.

00:52:33.530 --> 00:52:34.280
AUDIENCE: Hi, sir.

00:52:34.280 --> 00:52:36.680
Thank you for coming
and speaking tonight.

00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:41.212
Like you said earlier,
this issue isn't really

00:52:41.212 --> 00:52:42.920
getting any press,
and I know I certainly

00:52:42.920 --> 00:52:45.680
haven't heard of it anywhere.

00:52:45.680 --> 00:52:46.700
Why is that?

00:52:46.700 --> 00:52:49.280
Especially with the
attention that China

00:52:49.280 --> 00:52:52.160
is getting in this trade
war and everything.

00:52:52.160 --> 00:52:54.630
It would seem that
this would come up.

00:52:54.630 --> 00:52:55.800
So why is it so quiet?

00:52:59.085 --> 00:53:00.710
ROGER W. ROBINSON:
There's two reasons.

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:05.130
One, nobody seems to have
thought to do the research.

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:09.300
Nobody saw a national security
angle or a human rights angle

00:53:09.300 --> 00:53:13.240
to the cap markets
prior to ourselves.

00:53:13.240 --> 00:53:16.620
And it's a skill mix problem.

00:53:16.620 --> 00:53:18.540
I mean, when you
think about it, it's

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:21.390
useful to have some
Wall Street skills

00:53:21.390 --> 00:53:25.910
and some national
security skills.

00:53:25.910 --> 00:53:29.630
Well, the Wall Street
types don't really

00:53:29.630 --> 00:53:31.460
go for the national
security types,

00:53:31.460 --> 00:53:34.350
because they think they're
gratuitous disruptors.

00:53:34.350 --> 00:53:35.780
They're sanctions people.

00:53:35.780 --> 00:53:39.160
All they do is create
problems for them.

00:53:41.813 --> 00:53:43.730
On the other hand, the
national security types

00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:46.700
don't feel comfortable
talking about global finance

00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:50.120
in the capital markets because
their knowledge level might

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:54.170
be a millimeter deep, and
they're frantic with concern

00:53:54.170 --> 00:53:57.530
that some Master of the Universe
type from Goldman or Morgan

00:53:57.530 --> 00:54:02.270
Stanley is going to come on CNBC
and call them a neanderthal.

00:54:02.270 --> 00:54:03.560
Really.

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:09.560
So it's very rare that you have
a Wall Street-national security

00:54:09.560 --> 00:54:10.430
skill mix.

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:14.400
I've been in this business
for over 40 years.

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:17.150
And I can count the number
of folks with that skill mix

00:54:17.150 --> 00:54:21.060
that I've met
basically on one hand.

00:54:21.060 --> 00:54:22.960
In over 40 years.

00:54:22.960 --> 00:54:24.880
So we need a cadre.

00:54:24.880 --> 00:54:29.100
I mean, you know, we need
trained national security

00:54:29.100 --> 00:54:34.380
professionals who are
conversant and steeped

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:36.630
in global finance
and our markets.

00:54:36.630 --> 00:54:39.280
I'm not myself an
investment banker.

00:54:39.280 --> 00:54:41.250
You know, stocks and
bonds weren't my trade.

00:54:41.250 --> 00:54:45.510
I'm an international banker
with a commercial bank.

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:49.410
In other words, I had to
learn this kind of stuff.

00:54:49.410 --> 00:54:51.160
But it's not rocket science.

00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:52.530
It's all common sense.

00:54:52.530 --> 00:54:53.850
I mean, this is not--

00:54:53.850 --> 00:54:58.060
doesn't require a Wall
Street genius to do this.

00:54:58.060 --> 00:55:01.380
I mean, it's as straightforward
as the conversation

00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:02.900
we're having now.

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:04.950
It's a common sense thing.

00:55:04.950 --> 00:55:08.160
It's just that nobody
focused on the money.

00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:13.560
And in part, it's true that
that $40 to $60 trillion

00:55:13.560 --> 00:55:15.250
doesn't want you to
concentrate on it.

00:55:15.250 --> 00:55:17.610
China certainly doesn't.

00:55:17.610 --> 00:55:20.910
I would argue that the
Department of Treasury doesn't.

00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:24.240
And I wonder whether
the SEC does.

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:27.720
Because remember,
like the gentleman who

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:33.400
asked the earlier question,
you know, this is disruptive.

00:55:33.400 --> 00:55:36.190
This is not welcome.

00:55:36.190 --> 00:55:38.950
That's for sure.

00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:42.160
And that's a big
part of the problem

00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:45.730
as to why this thing never
has seen the light of day.

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:46.240
Yes.

00:55:46.240 --> 00:55:48.880
AUDIENCE: Would you
address the reciprocal

00:55:48.880 --> 00:55:53.635
in terms of China's investment
in US government bonds?

00:55:56.887 --> 00:55:58.470
ROGER W. ROBINSON:
I've got no problem

00:55:58.470 --> 00:56:01.680
with their ownership of
T-Bills, and for those

00:56:01.680 --> 00:56:04.290
who think that
their $1.1 trillion

00:56:04.290 --> 00:56:08.820
exposure in our T-Bills makes
us hostage to them in some way,

00:56:08.820 --> 00:56:10.710
and that they could
crater our economy,

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:15.120
and lots of these things
you hear, it's just not so.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:17.700
It would probably mean
a half a point to 100.

00:56:17.700 --> 00:56:23.280
I mean, it would be a 1%, half
a point to a point interest rate

00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:25.020
change.

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:27.130
It could raise our
rates somewhat.

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:29.410
But the notion that
a trillion dollars

00:56:29.410 --> 00:56:32.450
these days is going to
make that difference.

00:56:32.450 --> 00:56:34.510
And of course China
would lose their shirts,

00:56:34.510 --> 00:56:37.660
because obviously the
value of their portfolio

00:56:37.660 --> 00:56:40.210
would go through the floor.

00:56:40.210 --> 00:56:41.980
But we can handle it.

00:56:41.980 --> 00:56:46.360
I mean, how much did we spend
to get out of the 2007, 2009

00:56:46.360 --> 00:56:47.980
financial crisis?

00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:49.190
It wasn't $7 trillion.

00:56:49.190 --> 00:56:51.190
I think it was more like $15.

00:56:51.190 --> 00:56:52.570
Now, mind you, we were hurt.

00:56:52.570 --> 00:56:55.480
I mean, it halved my portfolio.

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:58.780
And we've taken a long time
to recover, so it hurt us.

00:56:58.780 --> 00:57:01.450
But a trillion is not
what it used to be,

00:57:01.450 --> 00:57:06.020
and China doesn't have any
meaningful leverage over us.

00:57:06.020 --> 00:57:09.470
SPEAKER: We have time
for one more question.

00:57:09.470 --> 00:57:13.718
AUDIENCE: Does President
Trump know this information?

00:57:13.718 --> 00:57:14.760
ROGER W. ROBINSON: Sorry?

00:57:14.760 --> 00:57:17.120
AUDIENCE: Does President
Trump know the information

00:57:17.120 --> 00:57:20.220
that we're hearing tonight?

00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:21.540
ROGER W. ROBINSON: No.

00:57:21.540 --> 00:57:24.990
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
President Arnn send him

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:27.820
to [INAUDIBLE] [? Phoebe. ?]

00:57:27.820 --> 00:57:29.570
ROGER W. ROBINSON: I
did a radio interview

00:57:29.570 --> 00:57:32.090
on this with Hillsdale today.

00:57:32.090 --> 00:57:35.750
That 15 or 20 minutes is pretty
incendiary, I can tell you.

00:57:35.750 --> 00:57:38.990
But look, I think
that the president

00:57:38.990 --> 00:57:44.010
is very steeped in the
markets in his own way.

00:57:44.010 --> 00:57:46.520
He's got the picture on this.

00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:48.805
Newt Gingrich did an--

00:57:48.805 --> 00:57:50.180
he's very close
to the president,

00:57:50.180 --> 00:57:52.490
he did an editorial
on this that I

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:56.300
worked with him on just recently
on this Thrift Savings Plan.

00:57:56.300 --> 00:57:59.150
That we have to reverse
that decision and why.

00:57:59.150 --> 00:58:02.150
I mean, on Wednesday
in the Post,

00:58:02.150 --> 00:58:04.040
there's a guy named
Josh [? Rogan ?]

00:58:04.040 --> 00:58:07.920
that's going to do a piece
that is just ruinous on this.

00:58:07.920 --> 00:58:10.820
We've got CNBC
interested in this.

00:58:10.820 --> 00:58:13.970
We've already had two
Financial Times pieces on this.

00:58:13.970 --> 00:58:15.920
They're going to go again.

00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:19.700
Rubio and Shaheen, in
a bipartisan letter,

00:58:19.700 --> 00:58:23.390
went after the Thrift
Savings Plan board on this,

00:58:23.390 --> 00:58:25.100
so it's already in the Congress.

00:58:25.100 --> 00:58:28.970
So again, this thing only
started to go public,

00:58:28.970 --> 00:58:33.410
with our research, in
mid-March of this year.

00:58:33.410 --> 00:58:37.190
So this is still
a nascent issue.

00:58:37.190 --> 00:58:39.080
It's going to take some time.

00:58:39.080 --> 00:58:41.360
But we don't have a lot
of time, as you can tell.

00:58:41.360 --> 00:58:43.970
I mean, this is a race.

00:58:43.970 --> 00:58:46.190
So we've got to get cracking.

00:58:46.190 --> 00:58:51.490
And anybody that can make
members of the administration,

00:58:51.490 --> 00:58:55.380
and the Congress, and even
your state legislatures,

00:58:55.380 --> 00:58:57.390
and even your
university endowments.

00:58:57.390 --> 00:59:00.240
I mean, this is an
awareness-raising time.

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:02.580
This is an educational time.

00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:04.080
And we've got to
step up, and we've

00:59:04.080 --> 00:59:08.100
got to step up fast, because
these guys are predators,

00:59:08.100 --> 00:59:09.880
and they are coming after us.

00:59:09.880 --> 00:59:10.380
Thank you.

00:59:10.380 --> 00:59:14.630
[APPLAUSE]

